{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>חֻקַּת עוֹלָם תְּחָגֻּהוּ.</b> The reason it was not sufficient to say ״לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם״ — and even though they of blessed memory said (Mekhilta) that ״דֹּרֹתֵיכֶם״ alone I would say [means] a minimum of two, therefore Scripture says ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם״ to reveal that the meaning of ״דֹּרֹתֵיכֶם״ is continuously — this is still difficult: he should not have said ״לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם״ but only ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם.״ However, it may be explained according to the one who says (Berakhot 12b) ״לְמַעַן תִּזְכֹּר אֶת יוֹם צֵאתְךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם כֹּל יְמֵי חַיֶּיךָ״ — to include the days of the Messiah — and according to the one who says ״כֹּל יְמֵי חַיֶּיךָ״ — the nights [as well]. It is possible that He said ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם״ so that you not say: it is well in the days of freedom, but in the days of exile when we are subjugated, what place is there for a remembrance of goodness that has ceased? Therefore Scripture says ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם״ — even in the time of subjugation in other exiles, HaShem has enacted a statute. And from the middle of this explanation you will also understand the removal of the objection to ״לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם״ — that its intent is not [merely] two generations. For if He had not said ״לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם,״ we would not have heard what we explained regarding ״חֻקַּת עוֹלָם,״ and we would have said that it comes only to make the generations continuous and not [merely] for two generations alone, which is what is heard from the phrasing ״לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם״ — and from the extra word we heard what we explained."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The phrase מֵאֶמְצָעוּת פֵּרוּשָׁם זֶה is translated hyper-literally as 'from the middle of this explanation'; the idiom likely means 'by inference from this explanation.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:02.996322988Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}